config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030
	bool "ar8030"
	depends on BR2_INSTALL_LIBSTDCPP
	depends on BR2_TOOLCHAIN_HAS_THREADS
	help
	  Artosyn AR8030 baseband host support — the vendor "yz_host_drv"
	  SDK's userspace RPC daemon + client library (userspace only; the
	  kernel driver is a separate concern, see below).

	  Always installed:
	  - ar8030d: the SDK "daemon". It owns the char device and serves
	    the RPC that applications talk to (TCP on 127.0.0.1 plus a unix
	    socket). Nothing else can reach the baseband while it is down,
	    so it must be started before any client.
	  - libar8030_client.so: the bb_* client API. Headers are installed
	    into <staging>/usr/include/ar8030.

	  Upstream calls these "daemon", "app", "ota" and "cmd_dbg"; they
	  are installed with an ar8030 prefix here so they can share
	  /usr/bin with the rest of the rootfs.

	  This package does not build a kernel module at all -- it used to
	  (the vendor SDK's own out-of-tree driver, USB+SDIO+DRV-mode in
	  one module, incrementally patched here to fix a series of real
	  bugs found over an extended investigation), but that driver is
	  gone in favor of a clean-room-rewritten replacement:
	  ar8030-transport's own kmod/artosyn_drv.c, built and installed by
	  the ar8030-transport-tx package instead. See that repo's
	  README.md ("Clean-room rewrite" section) for the full story of
	  why. Only SDIO-mode transport (daemon -i 1, /dev/artosyn_sdio) is
	  available as a result -- DRV-mode (-i 3, /dev/ar_mdev<N>) has no
	  kernel-side counterpart left to open.

	  The SDK is only published as source on a vendor Gitea instance
	  (no releases, no tags), so the package is pinned to a commit.

	  https://git.topxgun.com/czdu/yz_host_drv

comment "ar8030 needs a toolchain w/ C++, threads"
	depends on !BR2_INSTALL_LIBSTDCPP || !BR2_TOOLCHAIN_HAS_THREADS

if BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030

config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE
	bool "install the demo baseband firmware"
	default y
	help
	  Installs the vendor's demo baseband image + config to
	  /lib/firmware/ar8030 (ar8030.img, ar8030.json), so the kernel
	  module (ar8030-transport-tx's own artosyn_drv.ko) can boot the
	  chip with fw_name=/cfg_name= module parameters.

	  ar8030.json is committed; ar8030.img is fetched fresh at build
	  time instead, by whatever script BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE_
	  FETCH_SCRIPT names (below) -- it's a proprietary vendor binary
	  this repo does not commit. Best-effort: no network or a broken
	  fetch just means ar8030.img isn't installed that build, not a
	  build failure (ar8030-transport-tx's own S65 init script already
	  omits fw_name= gracefully when it's absent). Sensor/camera
	  tuning, if this board needs any, is a *different* package's
	  concern (e.g. waybeam) -- not this one's.

	  Turn this off if the AR8030 on your board boots from its own
	  flash.

config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE_FETCH_SCRIPT
	string "vendor firmware fetch script (path relative to the external tree)"
	depends on BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE
	help
	  Every AR8030-based board sources its demo baseband image from
	  its own vendor's stock firmware, in that vendor's own format --
	  this package deliberately carries none of that itself, so it
	  stays reusable across boards from different vendors. Set this in
	  your device's own defconfig to a script path relative to the
	  external tree root (the BR2_EXTERNAL variable, this build's
	  general/ directory), e.g. "scripts/fetch-vendor-firmware.py" for
	  a script living at your device's general/scripts/fetch-vendor-
	  firmware.py -- it gets copied there the same way general/
	  scripts/make-usrdata-image.sh does.

	  Called as: <script> <BINARIES_DIR> <output-dir>, before this
	  package builds. Must write ar8030.img into <output-dir> (nothing
	  else -- a sibling package handles any sensor tuning this board
	  also needs from the same vendor image), and must never fail the
	  build itself -- print a warning and leave <output-dir> empty on
	  any failure instead.

	  See devices/hi3516cv6xx_fpv_caddx-ascent-lite's defconfig +
	  general/scripts/fetch-vendor-firmware.py for a full example
	  (CADDX's "Ascent H Sky" OTA image on Google Drive). Left empty,
	  no fetch runs and ar8030.img doesn't get installed.

config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE_FETCH_DEPENDENCY
	string "extra package this fetch script needs built first (optional)"
	depends on BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE
	help
	  Optional Buildroot package name, added to AR8030_DEPENDENCIES,
	  that BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_FIRMWARE_FETCH_SCRIPT (above) needs
	  already built -- e.g. a device-owned package that does the
	  actual vendor download/extraction once and caches it, shared
	  with other packages that also need pieces of the same vendor
	  image (this device's own ascent-vendor-firmware, shared with
	  waybeam's sensor tuning, is the worked example). This is what
	  actually gets you "downloaded/extracted before this package
	  builds" with a real guarantee -- BR2_ROOTFS_PRE_BUILD_SCRIPT
	  looks like the obvious tool for that but is not wired into this
	  project's build (nothing make BOARD=... runs depends on it).

	  Leave empty if your fetch script is self-contained (does its own
	  fetching with no ordering requirement against another package).

config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_PAIR_TOOL
	bool "bb_pair binding tool"
	default y
	select BR2_PACKAGE_CJSON
	help
	  Installs bb_pair as /usr/bin/ar8030-pair: the vendor's
	  pair/bind utility. Talks to ar8030d over the local RPC socket,
	  so the daemon has to be running.

	  On a successful pair, also writes the peer's MAC into the
	  on-disk baseband config (see 0006-*.patch) so it survives the
	  next reboot's firmware push, and ar8030-transport-tx's own S65
	  init script spawns a background loop (see 0007-*.patch) that
	  keeps reconnecting to it at every boot -- so a paired unit no
	  longer needs to be re-paired by hand every time.

config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_USB_LOADER
	bool "USB rom-code firmware loader"
	default n
	help
	  Installs ar8030_usb_test_tool as /usr/bin/ar8030-usb-loader,
	  the userspace path for pushing a baseband image into an AR8030
	  sitting in USB rom-code mode (VID:PID 4152:8030), used together
	  with the SDK's mdev "autoload" hotplug flow.

	  Redundant when the kernel driver does the download itself, and
	  it statically links the SDK's bundled libusb, so it is off by
	  default.

config BR2_PACKAGE_AR8030_TOOLS
	bool "extra debug tools"
	default n
	help
	  Installs the SDK's demo and debug programs:
	    ar8030-cmd-dbg      interactive command console (cmd_dbg)
	    ar8030-ota          OTA image upgrade tool (ota)
	    ar8030-test         the generic API demo (app)
	    ar8030-netdev-demo  network-interface configuration demo

	  These are vendor test programs, not production tooling.

endif
